      "nets": ["SDA", "PWM", "SCK"],
      "observed": "Both U2 (ATtiny45-20P) and U3 (ATtiny13V-10S) are present in the netlist, and their corresponding GPIO pins share the SDA, PWM, and SCK nets. The netlist also connects both MCUs to the same reset, power, ground, POT, and TACH nets.",
      "expected": "A fan controller should use one active MCU, or clearly designate the two MCU footprints as mutually exclusive assembly options. No such exclusion is shown; shared MCU outputs can contend, and a common ISP connection can make programming ambiguous.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"PWM\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"6\") ...) (node (ref \"U3\") (pin \"6\") ...))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"SDA\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"5\") ...) (node (ref \"U3\") (pin \"5\") ...))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"SCK\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"7\") ...) (node (ref \"U3\") (pin \"7\") ...))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If both MCUs are populated, their GPIO outputs are electrically paralleled. Differing output states or timing can cause contention and unreliable PWM/control behavior; programming through the shared ISP connections may also fail or affect both devices.",
      "confidence": 0.92
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist includes two ATtiny MCUs connected in parallel across the controller’s signal and programming nets, with no indication that one is an unpopulated alternative. This can cause output contention and unreliable operation."
}

